  "answer": "The passages describe a quality-assurance framework, but not the detailed mechanics of checking individual examiners’ work.\n\n**Manual practice**\n• IP Australia uses a quality management system to monitor, manage and review the suitability and efficiency of its processes and procedures, with a focus on consistently delivering quality products and services. [TMM/Part1/2#1]\n• Quality Standards apply to all aspects of trade mark examination and provide benchmarks for assessing or reviewing the quality of completed work. This is the most direct answer to how examination quality is checked. [TMM/Part1/2#1]\n• Service Level Commitments set customer expectations for applications, examination requests and hearing decisions. [TMM/Part1/2#1]\n• The online Manual is the official controlled record of examination practice and procedures. It supports implementation of the quality standards, but remains a guide to be interpreted in the context of the Act, Regulations and relevant court and tribunal decisions—not legislation itself. [TMM/Part1/x-1.-introduction7#1; TMM/Part1/2#1]\n\n**Act requirement**\nSection 31 requires the Registrar, in accordance with the Regulations, to examine and report on whether an application complies with the Act and whether there are statutory grounds for rejection. This is the legal examination duty, distinct from the Manual’s quality-management framework. [TMA1995/s31]\n\n**Limit of the supplied material:** The quality passage identifies review benchmarks, but does not specify who checks examination work, how frequently it is checked, sampling arrangements or corrective procedures. I cannot establish those details from the passages provided. [TMM/Part1/2#1]"
